Breadth-first search explores a graph in rings: first the start node, then everything one edge away, then everything two edges away, and so on. Because it reaches nodes in order of distance, the first time BFS sees a node it has found a *shortest* path to it, counted in edges.

## A queue keeps the rings in order

Discovered nodes wait in a first-in, first-out queue. Take from the front, add undiscovered neighbours to the back — so every node at distance d leaves the queue before any node at distance d + 1:

:::viz{id=bfs-layers}

```python run
from collections import deque

def bfs(adj, start):
    dist = {start: 0}
    queue = deque([start])
    while queue:
        u = queue.popleft()
        for v in adj[u]:
            if v not in dist:          # first discovery = shortest distance
                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1
                queue.append(v)
    return dist

adj = {
    "A": ["B", "C"], "B": ["A", "D", "E"], "C": ["A", "E", "F"],
    "D": ["B", "H"], "E": ["B", "C", "G"], "F": ["C", "G"],
    "G": ["E", "F"], "H": ["D"],
}
print(bfs(adj, "A"))
```

:::callout{kind=pitfall title="Mark a node when it enters the queue"}
Record a node as seen when you enqueue it, not when you dequeue it. Otherwise a node reachable from several queued nodes is added several times — the distances stay right, but the queue and the running time can blow up.
:::

:::callout{kind=complexity time="O(V + E)" space="O(V)"}
Every node is enqueued once, and every edge is examined once from each end.
:::

## Grids and many starting points

On a grid, BFS from one cell counts the steps to every other cell. Start from *several* cells at once — put them all in the queue at distance 0 — and BFS computes every cell's distance to its nearest source: the idea behind problems like rotting oranges or flooding from many drains. When edges carry different weights BFS is no longer enough; that is Dijkstra's algorithm, in the heaps module.

:::question{slug=number-of-islands}

The hardest part of dynamic programming is choosing what a cell means. For the longest strictly increasing subsequence (its elements need not be adjacent), "the answer for the first i elements" does not compose — to extend a subsequence you need to know where it *ends*. So define the state by its last element:

$$
\text{dp}[i] = 1 + \max_{j < i,\; a_j < a_i} \text{dp}[j]
$$

(or 1 when no earlier element is smaller).

```python run
def lis_quadratic(a):
    dp = [1] * len(a)          # dp[i]: longest increasing run ending at a[i]
    for i in range(len(a)):
        for j in range(i):
            if a[j] < a[i]:
                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)
    return max(dp, default=0)

print(lis_quadratic([10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18]))  # 4: 2, 3, 7, 18
```

That is O(n²). A sharper state gives O(n log n): let `tails[k]` be the *smallest possible last element* of an increasing subsequence of length k + 1. `tails` is always sorted, so each new element finds its place by binary search — the lower bound from the Search & Order track:

```python run
from bisect import bisect_left

def lis(a):
    tails = []
    for x in a:
        k = bisect_left(tails, x)  # the first tail >= x
        if k == len(tails):
            tails.append(x)        # x extends the longest subsequence so far
        else:
            tails[k] = x           # x is a smaller ending for length k + 1
    return len(tails)

print(lis([10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18]))  # 4
print(lis(list(range(1000, 0, -1))))       # 1: strictly decreasing
```

:::callout{kind=pitfall title="tails is not the subsequence"}
`tails` has the right *length* but is usually not an actual increasing subsequence of the input — it mixes endings of different candidates. Reconstructing the sequence itself needs parent pointers.
:::

:::callout{kind=complexity time="O(n log n)" space="O(n)"}
n binary searches over a sorted list of at most n tails.
:::

:::question{slug=longest-increasing-subsequence}

BFS finds shortest paths when every edge costs 1. With weights, the node that is nearest by *edge count* may not be nearest by *cost*. Dijkstra's algorithm fixes that with a greedy rule and a heap: repeatedly settle the unsettled node with the smallest known distance, then try to improve its neighbours through it.

## The algorithm

```python run
import heapq

def dijkstra(adj, source):
    dist = {source: 0}
    heap = [(0, source)]                 # (distance so far, node)
    while heap:
        d, u = heapq.heappop(heap)
        if d > dist[u]:
            continue                     # stale entry: u was settled more cheaply
        for v, w in adj.get(u, []):
            nd = d + w
            if nd < dist.get(v, float("inf")):
                dist[v] = nd             # relax the edge u -> v
                heapq.heappush(heap, (nd, v))
    return dist

adj = {
    "S": [("A", 4), ("B", 1)],
    "B": [("A", 2), ("C", 5)],
    "A": [("C", 1)],
    "C": [("D", 3)],
}
print(dijkstra(adj, "S"))  # A is cheaper through B: 1 + 2 = 3
```

Relaxing an edge u → v with weight w applies:

$$
\text{dist}(v) \leftarrow \min\left(\text{dist}(v),\; \text{dist}(u) + w(u, v)\right)
$$

Why is a popped distance final? Every weight is ≥ 0, so any other route to u would leave the heap at a distance at least as large, and could only add cost after that. That is the greedy choice — and exactly what breaks when weights can be negative.

:::callout{kind=pitfall title="Stale heap entries"}
Python's heap cannot lower an existing key, so one node may sit in the heap several times with different distances. Skip any entry whose distance is worse than the best known (`d > dist[u]`) instead of processing it again.
:::

:::callout{kind=complexity time="O((V + E) log V)" space="O(V + E)"}
Each edge can push one heap entry, and every push or pop costs O(log V).
:::

:::callout{kind=note title="Negative weights"}
With a negative edge, a node settled early might later become cheaper, and Dijkstra never revisits it. Use Bellman–Ford, in O(V · E), when weights can be negative.
:::

